    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the multiple dwelling law, the emergency housing rent control law, the emergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-four, and the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to making technical corrections; and to repeal section 467-i of the real property tax law relating to real property tax abatement",
    "basePrintNo": "S6662",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6662-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §282-a, Mult Dwell L; amd §2, Emerg Hous Rent Cont L; amd §5, Emerg Ten Prot Act of 1974; amd §§26-403, 26-504.2 & 26-511, NYC Ad Cd; rpld §467-i, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Multiple Dwelling Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6662\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the multiple dwelling law, the emergency housing rent\ncontrol law, the emergency tenant protection act of nineteen\nseventy-four, and the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to making technical corrections; and to repeal section 467-i\nof the real property tax law relating to real property tax abatement\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend for two years the provisions of\nthe Loft Law and to make technical amendments to Chapter 20 of the\nLaws of 2015 in relation to the procedure for the deregulation of\nhousing accommodations subject to rent regulation in order to\neffectuate the intent of, and agreement between, the parties.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 282-a of the multiple dwelling\nlaw to extend the Loft Law for two years.\n\nSections 2-6 of this bill amend several provisions of the rent\nregulation laws relating to the deregulation of housing accommodations\nsubject to rent regulation. This bill eliminates any confusion\nrelating to the methodology for determining whether a housing\naccommodation is deregulated based upon the legal regulated rent as of\nthe date of such vacancy.\n\nSection 7 of the bill repeals section 467-I of the real property tax\nlaw.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill extends for two additional years all applications for\nregistration as an interim multiple dwelling under the Loft Law.\n\nThis bill also clarifies the statutory language relating to the\nderegulation of housing accommodations, the need for which arises due\nto technical errors in Chapter 20, and is necessary to ensure that the\nmethodology, which has been in effect since the enactment of the Rent\nRegulation Reform Act of 1997 relating to the deregulation of such\nhousing accommodations, continues in full force and effect.\n\nThis bill clarifies technical errors to provide that the statute is\nconsistent with the Division of Homes and Community Renewal's, lease\nrider, which was revised by HCR subsequent to the enactment of Chapter\n20, and which is required by law to be provided to tenants along with\nthe initial lease to the initial tenant subsequent to the prior\nvacancy.\n\nIncluded among the rider's provisions is a chart in which the owner\nsets forth the calculations to demonstrate to the first deregulated\ntenant the lawful increases subsequent to the prior vacancy resulting\nin the deregulation of the housing accommodation. Notwithstanding\nother modifications made to the lease rider arising from the enactment\nof Chapter 20, the same deregulation methodology that was contained in\nthe agency's rider prior to the enactment of Chapter 20 continues\nsubsequent to the enactment of Chapter 20. The rider remains unchanged\nfor the purposes of the methodology for deregulation. This bill makes\ntechnical changes to ensure consistency in the application of\nderegulation.\n\nAlso, the same 2015 law included two versions of a real property tax\nabatement available for New York City buildings subject to the\nEmergency Housing Rent Control Law or the New York City Rent and\nRehabilitation Law or the Emergency Tenant Protection Act of 1974.\n\nSection 64-a and Section 65 of the Chapter are the competing versions\nof the program. The New York State Department of Taxation and Finance\nissued a notice in October, 2015, stating, \"Until further notice,\nSection 65 is the operative real property tax abatement.\" (N-15-12)\nThis bill would repeal RPTL § 467-i enacted under Section 64-a of\nChapter 20 of the Laws of 2015.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill takes effect immediately and sections two through seven are\nintended to be retroactive in force and effect to the date of\nenactment of Chapter 20 of the Laws of 2015.\n",
